{"parentUuid":"bfc604e5-7f0e-4b78-a9fe-fde0c4a0a4e6","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"c3ec3062-afe7-4067-a7b7-47b42ffe60da","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/zenray/.claude/skills/kanji-stroke-order-html\n\n# Kanji Stroke Order — Full HTML study sheet\n\nGenerates a single self-contained HTML file per kanji, with the stroke-order SVG inlined directly inside the body (no external `<img src=\"...\">` reference — critical for Claude Code preview compatibility), plus a complete study template with all pedagogical metadata.\n\n## When to use\n\nUse this skill systematically when teaching a new kanji during a Japanese lesson with Matthieu. The output goes into `Memory/Japanese/KanjiProgress/<batch>/` with a strict naming convention so the kanji library scales cleanly to 2000+ items.\n\nTriggers:\n- A new kanji is taught in a Japanese lesson\n- Matthieu requests the HTML study sheet for a specific kanji\n- Drill / revision phase needs a clickable link to a previously-taught kanji (regenerate the HTML if it doesn't exist yet)\n\nFor raw SVG without the wrapper, use `kanji-stroke-order-svg`.\n\n## Architecture and naming convention\n\n```\nMemory/Japanese/KanjiProgress/\n├── 001-100 N5/         ← first 100 kanji, JLPT N5 level\n│   ├── 001 人 Hito Jin.html\n│   ├── 002 日 Hi Nichi.html\n│   └── ...\n├── 101-200 N4/         ← next batch\n└── ...\n```\n\n**File naming convention :** `NNN 漢 Kun On.html`\n- `NNN` = 3-digit chronological index (order of teaching in Japanese1.md, never reordered)\n- `漢` = the kanji itself (Unicode)\n- `Kun` = primary KUN reading in PascalCase (e.g. `Hito`, `Michi`, `Umi`)\n- `On` = primary ON reading in PascalCase (e.g. `Jin`, `Dou`, `Kai`)\n\nExamples : `059 何 Nani Nan.html` · `060 道 Michi Dou.html` · `061 海 Umi Kai.html`\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Generate the temporary SVG** with the bundled stroke_diagram script :\n   ```bash\n   python3 scripts/stroke_diagram.py <character> -o /tmp/kanji_tmp.svg [--cols N]\n   ```\n2. **Read** `/tmp/kanji_tmp.svg`\n3. **Strip the XML declaration** (`<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>`) so the SVG embeds cleanly inside HTML\n4. **Build the final HTML** by injecting the cleaned SVG inside the `<div class=\"strokes\">` block of the template (below) and filling all metadata placeholders\n5. **Save** to `Memory/Japanese/KanjiProgress/<batch>/<NNN> <漢> <Kun> <On>.html`\n6. **Delete the temporary SVG** — the final HTML is self-sufficient, no orphan files\n\n## HTML template\n\nUse this template verbatim, replacing the `{{PLACEHOLDERS}}` :\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"fr\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>{{NNN}} — {{KANJI}} ({{SENS}})</title>\n<style>\n  body { font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"Segoe UI\", sans-serif; max-width: 980px; margin: 2rem auto; padding: 0 1.5rem; color: #1a1a1a; line-height: 1.55; }\n  header { border-bottom: 2px solid #1a1a1a; padding-bottom: 0.8rem; margin-bottom: 1.4rem; }\n  h1 { margin: 0; font-size: 2.2rem; font-weight: 600; }\n  .meta { color: #555; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-top: 0.4rem; }\n  .meta span { margin-right: 1.2rem; }\n  .strokes { background: #fafafa; border: 1px solid #e8e8e8; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 1.4rem; }\n  .strokes svg { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }\n  h2 { font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 600; margin-top: 1.6rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: #2a2a2a; }\n  .mnemo { background: #fff8e6; padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem; border-left: 3px solid #d4a017; border-radius: 4px; }\n  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin-top: 0.4rem; }\n  td { padding: 0.4rem 0.6rem; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee; }\n  td:first-child { font-weight: 500; width: 35%; }\n  .phrase { background: #f0f4ff; padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem; border-left: 3px solid #4a6fa5; border-radius: 4px; font-size: 1.05rem; }\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<header>\n  <h1>{{NNN}} — {{KANJI}} · {{SENS}}</h1>\n  <div class=\"meta\">\n    <span><strong>ON :</strong> {{ON}}</span>\n    <span><strong>KUN :</strong> {{KUN}}</span>\n    <span><strong>{{TRAITS}} traits</strong></span>\n    <span><strong>Radical :</strong> {{RADICAL}}</span>\n  </div>\n</header>\n\n<div class=\"strokes\">\n{{SVG_INLINED_HERE}}\n</div>\n\n<h2>Mnémo</h2>\n<div class=\"mnemo\">\n  {{MNEMO_HTML}}\n</div>\n\n<h2>Exemples</h2>\n<table>\n{{EXAMPLES_ROWS}}\n</table>\n\n<h2>Phrase</h2>\n<div class=\"phrase\">{{PHRASE_JP}} — <em>{{PHRASE_FR}}</em></div>\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\nOptional sections to add between `Mnémo` and `Exemples` when useful :\n- **`<h2>Lecture — règle de switch</h2>`** for kanji like 何 with reading switch rules\n- **`<h2>Détail tracé</h2>`** for complex stroke orders\n- **`<h2>Combo découverte</h2>`** when the kanji links nicely with previously-taught kanjis\n\n## Critical rule — ONE FILE ONLY\n\n**Never produce two files (HTML + separate SVG).** The Claude Code preview does not follow external file references, so a `<img src=\"external.svg\">` would display as a broken image icon. The SVG content must be inlined directly inside the HTML body.\n\nIf you accidentally wrote an `<img>` tag, the fix is :\n1. Read the external SVG file\n2. Strip its XML declaration\n3. Replace the `<img src=\"...\">` tag with the SVG content\n4. Delete the external SVG file\n\n## Coverage and attribution\n\nSame as `kanji-stroke-order-svg` : KanjiVG covers all jōyō, jinmeiyō, hiragana and katakana. Rare CJK extension characters may be missing.\n\nKanjiVG data © Ulrich Apel and contributors, CC BY-SA 3.0.\n\n\nARGUMENTS: il manque pour les derniers Kanji dans KanjiProgress"}]},"isMeta":true,"uuid":"011c7242-ffca-4d5a-aeb0-8bd5ea370d6d","timestamp":"2026-06-02T14:36:06.542Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/Create/Build/Memory","sessionId":"ee7eedf5-c4db-4ffa-b0da-0fd95dbb0ba8","version":"2.1.160","gitBranch":"main","slug":"snuggly-crunching-trinket"}
